A||||3||fyerstarter||Who do Ignition Modules Fail?||||1||
Z||000000||fyerstarter||05-13-2001||11:49 AM||||Ok first thanks to the forum member who recommended carrying a spare ignition module in your car. You rule dude!<P>So anyway I was driving home last night on the freeway and BLAM, the car just died and wouldn't start. Ignition module went out again! Luckily 2 days before I had just recieved my Accell Module and threw it in the trunk because the car was acting flaky. <P>So needless to say it was the module that fried AGAIN! And an hour later (i didnt have a flashlight) I was back on the road. <P>The ignition module was replaced only 2 months previous to this. So I was wondering is there anything I can check for that could be frying my igniton modules? Or is it just another Fiero mystery why these things always go out.<P>Could it be:<P>-The first module I got was a cheapy Autozone one (maybe that was just crap)?<P>-Timing off?<P>-ECM?<P>-Bad wiring or something grounding out somewhere ?<P>-The car is possessed?<P>-Bad pickup coil or distributer? <P>Any ideas?<P>Thanks<P>-E.<p>[This message has been edited by fyerstarter (edited 05-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Vader||05-13-2001||12:04 PM||||Fyer,<P>Usually it is due to excessive primary coil current, and/or the inability of the module to dissipate heat from the current.  You must use silicone heat sink grease to mount the module, and the mating surfaces must be clean and smooth before installing the module.<P>It is possible that the ignition coil primary winding is shorting, or that you have an intermittent short to ground in the coil primary wiring.  <P><I>(In order of presentation <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></I><P>1.  You may have had a defective or inferior module;<P>2.  The ignition timing has no effect on the primary coil current, and therefore no effect on the life of the module;  (O.K., for those of you being anal about it, there might be a slight increase in primary current at highly advanced initial timing due to the requirement for slightly higher spark energy in a cylinder with slightly higher chamber pressure, but the difference would be in teh milliamps on the primary side - nothing significant.)<P>3.  Ditto the ECM, since it only introduces retard steps in timing if the car has an EST circuit.  The only difference is the spark timing, not energy;<P>4.  Final answer?  DING! DING!  You might be a winner;<P>5.  Does the grille badge say "Ford" on it?<P>6.  The pickup coil only introduces a very low-level trigger pulse to the detector circiut in the amplifier/switch module.  The chances of the voltage from teh trigger array being high enough to damage teh module are about equal to my chances of winning teh lottery three weeks in a row, but anything is possible;<P>7.  See above;<P>8.  You're quite welcome.<BR><P>------------------<BR>"<I>Make Me Bad</I>"||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierospeeder||05-13-2001||05:37 PM||||Are your sparkplugs gapped correctly??<P>Are there any cracks in your plug wires?||||reg||1
Z||000003||fyerstarter||05-13-2001||06:42 PM||||Thanks for the info Vadar. I'm just going to assume that I got a bad coil. I used silicone grease on the surface both times (the one that fried and this one). Maybe it was a bad coil but I just put in my MSD Blaster Coil today so I hope it's all good. <P>Yes the spark plugs are gapped correctly and the wires are in good condition.||||reg||1
